1
00:00:00,033 --> 00:00:02,250
One dollar and fifty-four cents for Haiku,

2
00:00:02,250 --> 00:00:04,417
twelve dollars and twenty-two
cents for Sonnet,

3
00:00:04,417 --> 00:00:07,133
and nearly thirty dollars for Opus:

4
00:00:07,133 --> 00:00:10,367
is it actually worth paying twenty times
more when coding with Claude?

5
00:00:10,567 --> 00:00:15,650
We ran eighteen isolated benchmarks
across all three tiers of Claude 5.5

6
00:00:15,650 --> 00:00:20,017
and the unthinkable happened:
Opus swept the first half three to zero,

7
00:00:20,017 --> 00:00:22,983
but Haiku and Sonnet stole
the final three challenges.

8
00:00:23,017 --> 00:00:27,617
Today we climb the entire Claude ladder
across six extreme engineering challenges

9
00:00:27,617 --> 00:00:31,167
to find out exactly where you
should spend your money.

10
00:00:34,767 --> 00:00:36,833
I'm Kevin, this is Mixiuh Labs,

11
00:00:36,833 --> 00:00:40,700
and every single week developers ask me
whether it still makes sense to pay

12
00:00:40,700 --> 00:00:44,900
flagship Opus pricing when Sonnet
and Haiku are so fast.

13
00:00:44,933 --> 00:00:46,767
To answer with hard data,

14
00:00:46,767 --> 00:00:50,883
we ran the exact same one-shot
prompt across all three models:

15
00:00:50,883 --> 00:00:56,650
first you'll see Haiku and Sonnet side
by side, followed by Opus in full screen.

16
00:00:56,650 --> 00:01:00,350
Our refereeing rule is strict:
we score real physics accuracy,

17
00:01:00,350 --> 00:01:04,900
zero console errors, and return
on investment per dollar spent on the API.

18
00:01:04,933 --> 00:01:08,583
In total, Haiku finished all
six challenges in 87 minutes for a dollar

19
00:01:08,583 --> 00:01:14,100
fifty-four, Sonnet in 105 minutes
for twelve dollars and twenty-two cents,

20
00:01:14,100 --> 00:01:17,850
and Opus in 179 minutes
for nearly thirty dollars.

21
00:01:17,883 --> 00:01:22,483
Let's jump into all six extreme challenges
to see where each tier of Claude shines

22
00:01:22,483 --> 00:01:24,367
and where it stumbles.

23
00:01:24,750 --> 00:01:29,750
Challenge 1: build a Martian Rover
suspension testbed with an articulated

24
00:01:29,750 --> 00:01:33,500
chassis, Hooke's law spring-dampers,
deformable terrain,

25
00:01:33,500 --> 00:01:35,033
and live telemetry graphs.

26
00:01:35,067 --> 00:01:39,133
On the left, Haiku surprised us
by delivering a working 60 FPS rover

27
00:01:39,133 --> 00:01:42,900
in 19 minutes for just 35 cents;
but on the right,

28
00:01:42,900 --> 00:01:47,933
Sonnet spent two dollars and fifty-one
cents and truncated its script,

29
00:01:47,933 --> 00:01:49,317
leaving a black screen.

30
00:01:49,350 --> 00:01:54,683
When Opus 5.5 takes over, the engineering
leap is massive: it spent 42 minutes

31
00:01:54,683 --> 00:01:59,633
and 6 dollars and 81 cents to build
a rope-plank suspension bridge,

32
00:01:59,633 --> 00:02:03,317
lunar craters, volumetric headlights,
and live force vectors.

33
00:02:03,350 --> 00:02:07,567
While Haiku deserves credit
for running at 60 FPS for 35 cents,

34
00:02:07,567 --> 00:02:11,267
Opus's suspension bridge physics
and telemetry are in another league:

35
00:02:11,267 --> 00:02:13,833
point one goes to Opus 5.5.

36
00:02:14,217 --> 00:02:18,833
Challenge 2: simulate a biological
Physarum slime mold in WebGL2 where tens

37
00:02:18,833 --> 00:02:22,817
of thousands of GPU agents form
optimal rail networks by following

38
00:02:22,817 --> 00:02:24,083
pheromone trails.

39
00:02:24,117 --> 00:02:27,567
Here Haiku cost just 6 cents in 5 minutes,

40
00:02:27,567 --> 00:02:33,417
but failed to compile its GLSL shader
due to a vec3-to-vec4 dimension mismatch;

41
00:02:33,417 --> 00:02:37,200
while Sonnet hit 60 FPS for 79 cents

42
00:02:37,200 --> 00:02:40,633
but overexposed the center
into a white blob.

43
00:02:40,667 --> 00:02:47,467
Opus 5.5 spent 13 minutes and 2 dollars
and 55 cents to map 28 real Tokyo Bay

44
00:02:47,467 --> 00:02:50,733
rail stations from Shinjuku
to Yokohama, linking them

45
00:02:50,733 --> 00:02:55,417
with 60 thousand bioluminescent
GPU agents at 60 FPS.

46
00:02:55,450 --> 00:02:58,283
On scientific WebGL2
ping-pong float shaders,

47
00:02:58,283 --> 00:03:02,917
Opus proves why it's the flagship
tier and takes a two to zero lead.

48
00:03:03,300 --> 00:03:06,750
Challenge 3: a civil engineering
bridge simulator where all three models

49
00:03:06,750 --> 00:03:10,167
had to span a canyon,
drive heavy vehicles across,

50
00:03:10,167 --> 00:03:13,617
and snap steel beams when tension
exceeds the elastic limit.

51
00:03:13,650 --> 00:03:18,433
In this round all three models
delivered smooth 60 FPS simulators:

52
00:03:18,433 --> 00:03:22,783
Haiku built a Warren truss
with a 20-ton truck for 30 cents,

53
00:03:22,783 --> 00:03:26,867
and Sonnet animated a four-car heavy
freight train for a dollar ninety-eight.

54
00:03:26,900 --> 00:03:33,083
However, Opus 5.5 spent 19 minutes
and 3 dollars and 58 cents to code

55
00:03:33,083 --> 00:03:39,083
a 20-substep XPBD solver with live stress
percentages printed on every single beam,

56
00:03:39,083 --> 00:03:41,767
underwater piers,
and millimeter deflection readouts.

57
00:03:41,767 --> 00:03:45,350
For its CAD-grade precision
with per-beam stress readouts,

58
00:03:45,350 --> 00:03:48,550
Opus earns its third straight point;

59
00:03:48,550 --> 00:03:52,933
but watch closely,
because from here on everything flips.

60
00:03:53,317 --> 00:03:58,550
Challenge 4: an Acid Techno music
studio with a TB-303 bassline synth,

61
00:03:58,550 --> 00:04:04,817
TR-909 drum machine in Web Audio API,
cutoff and distortion rotary knobs,

62
00:04:04,817 --> 00:04:07,083
and an audio-reactive 3D tunnel.

63
00:04:07,117 --> 00:04:10,417
And here came the biggest
upset of the tournament:

64
00:04:10,417 --> 00:04:14,167
in just 8 and a half minutes
and for only 21 cents,

65
00:04:14,167 --> 00:04:18,550
Claude Haiku 5.5 built a 3D spectral
particle terrain, oscilloscope,

66
00:04:18,550 --> 00:04:22,583
7 rotary knobs,
and the full sequencer at 61 FPS.

67
00:04:22,617 --> 00:04:27,100
Sonnet delivered a wireframe holodome
for a dollar fifty-two and Opus built

68
00:04:27,100 --> 00:04:32,650
a slick laser highway, but Opus took
31 minutes and charged 5 full dollars:

69
00:04:32,650 --> 00:04:37,833
twenty-four times more expensive
than Haiku for the exact same frame rate.

70
00:04:37,867 --> 00:04:41,583
Three and a half times faster
and twenty-four times cheaper

71
00:04:41,583 --> 00:04:48,100
without dropping a single frame:
a historic ROI point for Claude Haiku 5.5.

72
00:04:48,483 --> 00:04:54,167
Challenge 5: code a Monte Carlo Path
Tracer from scratch in WebGL2 with BVH

73
00:04:54,167 --> 00:04:59,667
acceleration, glass refraction, caustics,
and a real-time A-Trous wavelet denoiser.

74
00:04:59,700 --> 00:05:01,900
On the left, Haiku built a basic
noisy tracer for 13 cents;

75
00:05:01,900 --> 00:05:07,150
but on the right, Claude Sonnet 5.5 shone
in just 13.9 minutes for a dollar

76
00:05:07,150 --> 00:05:10,750
sixty-seven,

77
00:05:10,750 --> 00:05:14,850
rendering rainbow chromatic
dispersion through glass at 29 FPS.

78
00:05:14,867 --> 00:05:20,150
Meanwhile, Opus 5.5 took more than twice
as long—31 minutes and 5 dollars

79
00:05:20,150 --> 00:05:24,767
and 5 cents—and its overly heavy
shader dragged performance down

80
00:05:24,767 --> 00:05:26,950
to just 15 frames per second.

81
00:05:26,983 --> 00:05:31,267
Nearly double the frame rate, in less
than half the time, at one-third the cost:

82
00:05:31,267 --> 00:05:35,317
a decisive victory for Claude Sonnet 5.5.

83
00:05:35,700 --> 00:05:41,767
Final challenge: build an emulator and IDE
for the classic CHIP-8 console complete

84
00:05:41,767 --> 00:05:46,483
with a 35-opcode CPU, phosphor
CRT monitor, live disassembler,

85
00:05:46,483 --> 00:05:48,283
hex dump, and custom assembler.

86
00:05:48,317 --> 00:05:49,333
Look at the split screen:

87
00:05:49,333 --> 00:05:55,617
both Haiku for 48 cents and Sonnet
for 3 dollars and 75 cents booted straight

88
00:05:55,617 --> 00:06:02,050
into live Pong at 60 FPS, with Sonnet
adding a curved CRT bezel, register bars,

89
00:06:02,050 --> 00:06:05,000
and a syntax-highlighted ASM editor.

90
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:08,633
And here came Opus 5.5's
most painful stumble:

91
00:06:08,633 --> 00:06:11,933
after 42 minutes and nearly
7 dollars in tokens,

92
00:06:11,933 --> 00:06:14,817
it forgot to invoke
its ROM assembler on boot,

93
00:06:14,817 --> 00:06:19,350
leaving memory at zero bytes
and the CRT screen dark.

94
00:06:19,383 --> 00:06:25,233
Sonnet 5.5 takes the final point
with a flawless retro workstation,

95
00:06:25,233 --> 00:06:30,600
bringing the final Claude ladder
scoreboard to three points for Opus,

96
00:06:30,600 --> 00:06:33,917
two for Sonnet, and one for Haiku.

97
00:06:34,300 --> 00:06:36,550
Three for Opus, two for Sonnet,
and one for Haiku:

98
00:06:36,550 --> 00:06:41,483
this benchmark proves that paying
for the most expensive tier

99
00:06:41,483 --> 00:06:44,117
does not guarantee
winning every challenge.

100
00:06:46,517 --> 00:06:48,083
On the total stopwatch,

101
00:06:48,083 --> 00:06:52,850
Haiku finished all six challenges
in 87 minutes and Sonnet in 105,

102
00:06:52,850 --> 00:06:58,617
while Opus needed 179 minutes—nearly
three full hours of compute.

103
00:06:58,650 --> 00:07:02,317
In reliability, no tier
was infallible in a single shot:

104
00:07:02,317 --> 00:07:07,117
Haiku missed one GLSL cast, Sonnet
truncated one script, and Opus forgot

105
00:07:07,117 --> 00:07:11,583
to auto-assemble its ROM in Round 6 after
burning nearly seven dollars.

106
00:07:11,767 --> 00:07:14,283
And the API bill changes everything:

107
00:07:14,283 --> 00:07:18,400
one dollar and fifty-four cents
for the entire tournament on Haiku,

108
00:07:18,400 --> 00:07:20,350
twelve dollars
and twenty-two cents on Sonnet,

109
00:07:20,350 --> 00:07:23,750
and twenty-nine dollars
and ninety-four cents on Opus.

110
00:07:24,133 --> 00:07:31,100
Here is the golden rule for your wallet:
use Haiku 5.5 for rapid prototypes,

111
00:07:31,100 --> 00:07:36,217
UI dashboards, and audio synths
where it costs twenty times less.

112
00:07:36,250 --> 00:07:42,317
Use Sonnet 5.5 as your daily workhorse
for emulators, optimized shaders,

113
00:07:42,317 --> 00:07:47,867
and full web apps; and reserve Opus
5.5 strictly for multi-pass physics

114
00:07:47,867 --> 00:07:49,550
and heavy architectural math.

115
00:07:49,550 --> 00:07:53,733
I uploaded all eighteen interactive
demos, exact prompts,

116
00:07:53,733 --> 00:07:58,683
and token breakdowns to labs dot mixiuh
dot online so you can test all three tiers

117
00:07:58,683 --> 00:08:00,583
side by side in your browser.

118
00:08:00,617 --> 00:08:04,883
Subscribe to Mixiuh Labs, share
this benchmark with your team, and tell me

119
00:08:04,883 --> 00:08:08,717
in the comments which model family
we should put on the ladder in Episode 05.

120
00:08:09,100 --> 00:08:10,383
Experiment 04 complete:

121
00:08:10,383 --> 00:08:13,950
see you in the next level,
right here on Mixiuh Labs.
